# Target C-II — occurrence-injective transverse selection on long sides

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** `234bd432e4d3ebf2cf113d214be9e6188644449b`  
**Status:** proved proof-only strengthening of the low-`t` long-side branch; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** no-container Type-II full positive-contact core  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The angular-sector multiplicity theorem shows that one physical vertex supports at
most two `beta`-straight outer-face occurrences when `beta<=pi/6`. The preceding
count then chose only one transverse extra contact per physical branching vertex.
That is still unnecessarily lossy.

At a branching vertex with two distinct straight outer-face occurrences, the two
occurrences have different pairs of boundary edges. Since every non-route contact is
strongly transverse for the corresponding occurrence, one can choose **different**
extra contact edges for the two occurrences. Thus branching occurrences inject into
transverse contact-edge incidences at each physical vertex. Globally only the ordinary
two-endpoint multiplicity of an undirected edge remains.

This raises the rounded-long-side contact floor from `0.097613D` to `0.195226D`.

## 1. Local selection lemma

Fix a physical contact vertex `x` of full contact degree `d>=3`. Suppose `x` supports
`r` distinct `beta`-straight internal occurrences on the distinguished outer-face
routes, with

\[
0<\beta\le\pi/6.
\]

The angular-sector lemma gives

\[
r\le2.
\tag{OI.1}
\]

For one occurrence `o`, let `P_o` be the unordered pair of its predecessor and
successor physical contact edges at `x`. These two edges bound the corresponding
outer-face sector. Define

\[
A_o:=E_C(x)\setminus P_o,
\]

the contact edges at `x` not used by that route occurrence.

Every edge in `A_o` is strongly transverse relative to the occurrence. Indeed, its
contact ray is separated by strictly more than `pi/3` from both route-contact rays,
while those route rays lie within `beta` of the two opposite tangent directions.
Therefore every `e in A_o` has

\[
|\operatorname{trans}_{u_o}(e)|
>\sin(\pi/3-\beta).
\tag{OI.2}
\]

### Lemma OI.1 — distinct representatives for straight occurrences

There is an injective choice

\[
o\longmapsto e_o\in A_o
\]

for the `r` straight occurrences at `x`.

### Proof

If `r=1`, this is immediate because `d>=3` makes `A_o` nonempty.

Assume `r=2`. Distinct outer-face occurrences at a vertex correspond to distinct
angular face sectors. Their boundary-edge pairs `P_1,P_2` are therefore different.
For `d=3`, each allowed set `A_j` is a singleton. If those singletons were equal, their
complementary two-edge sets `P_1,P_2` would be equal, contradiction. Hence the two
singletons are distinct.

For `d>=4`, both `A_1` and `A_2` have size at least two. Two nonempty sets of size at
least two automatically satisfy Hall's condition for a two-element index set, so
distinct representatives exist. QED.

No KKT coefficient is used. The lemma uses only the cyclic rotation at a plane-graph
vertex and the triangle-free angular separation of unit contacts.

## 2. Global occurrence-to-edge count

Let

- `S_beta` be the total number of `beta`-straight internal long-side route
  occurrences;
- `S_2(beta)` be the subcount occurring at physical contact-degree-two vertices;
- `E_perp(beta)` be the number of distinct physical contact edges selected by applying
  Lemma OI.1 independently at every branching physical vertex.

Lemma OI.1 chooses one distinct edge incidence at a branching vertex for every one of
its straight occurrences. Hence the total number of selected edge incidences is
exactly

\[
S_\beta-S_2(\beta).
\]

One undirected physical contact edge has only two endpoints, so it can appear in at
most two selected incidences. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
2E_\perp(\beta)
\ge S_\beta-S_2(\beta).
}
\tag{OI.3}
\]

For the degree-two subcount, every such physical vertex is loaded by C26, is a
non-distinguished repeated outer-face occurrence, and is therefore an outer-bridge
vertex. The sector multiplicity theorem gives at most two straight occurrences per
such vertex, while bridge incidence gives `V_2(beta)<=B_o`. Thus

\[
S_2(\beta)\le2B_o.
\tag{OI.4}
\]

Combining (OI.3)--(OI.4),

\[
\boxed{
2B_o+2E_\perp(\beta)\ge S_\beta.
}
\tag{OI.5}
\]

This is the sharpest occurrence-safe conversion presently obtained from the local
outer-face geometry. The only remaining multiplicity factor is the unavoidable fact
that a physical contact edge has two endpoints.

## 3. Insert directional concentration and bridge suppression

The exact outer-route directional-energy theorem gives

\[
S_\beta
\ge
L_{>2}-{2d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta}.
\tag{OI.6}
\]

For the full-core Type-II branch, bridge-subtracted isoperimetry and
`P_F/D->pi` give along every convergent subsequence

\[
\limsup {2B_o\over D}\le\delta,
\qquad
\delta:=\lim {d_{out}\over D}.
\tag{OI.7}
\]

Hence for `beta<=pi/6`,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {2E_\perp(\beta)\over D}
\ge
\liminf {L_{>2}\over D}
-\delta\left(1+{2\over1-\cos\beta}\right).
}
\tag{OI.8}
\]

Every selected contact satisfies the directional bound (OI.2).

## 4. Current low-`t` long-side consequence

At the current proof-only floor `t=t_42`, the long-side alternative and low-detour
squeeze give

\[
{L_{>2}\over D}
\ge0.4363893718701161781\ldots-o(1),
\]

\[
\delta\le0.002883975.
\]

Set

\[
\beta={\pi\over6}.
\]

Then

\[
{S_{\pi/6}\over D}
\ge0.3933368069590823665\ldots-o(1),
\]

and (OI.5)--(OI.7) yield

\[
\boxed{
{E_\perp(\pi/6)\over D}
\ge0.1952264159795411832\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{OI.9}
\]

Every selected edge has absolute transverse component strictly greater than `1/2`
relative to the corresponding long loaded-hull side direction.

This is a factor-five improvement over the first bridge-suppressed estimate
`0.039045D` and a factor-two improvement over the sector-only estimate
`0.097613D`.

## 5. Updated low-`t` normal form

At the present floor, every full-core Type-II survivor now has one of two linear
physical contact populations of almost the same scale:

```text
tooth branch:
    >= 0.2127872328 D-o(D) distinct inward unit contacts,
    inward component > 0.5011115;

rounded long-side branch:
    >= 0.19522641598 D-o(D) distinct transverse unit contacts,
    transverse component > 1/2.
```

Thus every low-`t` survivor has at least

\[
\boxed{0.19522641597954118\ldots D-o(D)}
\]

distinct physical contacts belonging to one of two explicit strongly non-tangential
families.

The remaining obstruction is no longer a counting or boundary-repetition issue. A
closure theorem must explain how nearly `0.2D` such non-tangential contacts can be
routed through the pentagon/rhombus train network while preserving the actual diameter
thrackle and KKT equilibrium. The square-strip regression shows that one long train
can absorb many boundary transverse contacts, so the next target must split train
occupancy: many stopped trains versus long coherent rhombic strips.

## 6. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_long_side_occurrence_injective_selection.py
```

for the Decimal constant checks.
